\chapter{Packaging \whyse{}}
Installing an \textsc{Emacs~LISP} package is quite easy if the system is
distributed through the \textsc{GNU~Emacs~LISP~Package~Archive}
(\textsc{GNU~ELPA}), and only slightly less easy if it is distributed
through \textsc{MELPA}
(\textsc{Milkypostman's~Emacs~Lisp~Package~Archive}). Other package
archives have existed, but they are all ephemeral. The most popular
alternative to \textsc{GNU~ELPA}, \textsc{Non-GNU~ELPA}, and
\textsc{MELPA} is direct distribution of files through \textsc{Git}
servers and the use of a package by the end user to install directly
from such (e.g. \textsc{Quelpa}).

This software is in-development, so it will only be distributed directly
through \textsc{Git}.

\whyse{} follows the form of single-file packages documented in the
\textit{Emacs Lisp Reference Manual}. The package file, \whyseel{}, is
emitted by \texttt{notangle} which is called by the Makefile in every
target but [[clean]]. All source development occurs in \whysenw{} using
\textsc{Polymode}.

The makefile distributed alongside \whysenw{} in the tarball contains the
command-line used to tangle and weave \whyse{}.

